# act (nektos/act) — running these workflows locally, e.g. `act push -j build`.
#
# ⚠️ SAFETY — WHY THESE TWO LINES EXIST. DO NOT DELETE THEM.
#
# act reads `.env` from the repository root BY DEFAULT and injects every entry
# into the job container, where it takes precedence over the `env:` block the
# workflow itself declares. This repo's `.env` is gitignored and local-only, but
# it holds the LIVE Neon credentials — SUPERUSER_DSN, DATABASE_URL_MIGRATOR and
# DATABASE_URL_RUNTIME.
#
# api-ci's `integration` job declares those three as localhost:5432 pointing at
# its own throwaway postgres service. Unguarded, act overrides all three with the
# production values, and the job then runs — against the database serving the
# live event — the superuser role bootstrap (`psql -f api/db/init/00_roles.sql`),
# `goose up`, and the reversibility gate's `goose down-to 0`. The last of those
# drops every table.
#
# This was observed on 2026-08-10. It failed only because the network this project
# is developed on blocks outbound 5432, so psql was refused before it connected —
# see docs/deployment-runbook.md and the db-migrate workflow header for that same
# restriction. A hotspot or any other network removes the accident that saved it.
#
# These workflows need NO ambient configuration: every value they use is a CI-only
# literal declared in the workflow file (`ci_only_password`, the `*_dev_only_change_me`
# role passwords, a dev card-seal key). So loading nothing is not a limitation.
#
# If a local run ever genuinely needs a credential, pass `--env-file` / `--secret-file`
# explicitly on the command line for that one run. Never by deleting these lines.
--env-file=/dev/null
--secret-file=/dev/null
